# Summary
Introduced authorization gap: PutObjectAcl policy evaluation omits request ACL/grant details, so bucket policy conditions on x-amz-acl/x-amz-grant-* are ineffective and can be bypassed.
The commit adds bucket-policy authorization for PutObjectAcl/PutObjectVersionAcl, but the evaluation path uses bucket_policy_decision_for_object, which only supplies existing/request tags to PolicyRequest. It never populates x-amz-acl or x-amz-grant-* values from the PutObjectAcl request. Because bucket policies now support those condition keys for evaluable object actions, explicit Allow/Deny rules that depend on ACL/grant headers will not match. A delegated principal allowed to call PutObjectAcl can therefore bypass policy conditions (e.g., a deny on public-read) and set more permissive ACLs than intended, potentially exposing objects publicly or granting access to other accounts.

