## Report
Rubric-guided validation: I first attempted dynamic validation (compiled stack). `cargo test -p auth --lib` built and ran 299 tests successfully (no crash). Valgrind is unavailable (`valgrind --version` -> command not found). gdb is unavailable; lldb is available and I ran a single test under lldb with no crash (process exited 0, bt unavailable once exited). 

Code analysis shows the authorization gap. `bucket_policy_decision_for_object` constructs a `PolicyRequest` with only existing/request object tags (authz.rs:483-526) and never sets canned ACL or grant fields. The PutObjectAcl path uses this function: `requester_can_write_object_acl_with_bucket_policy` calls `bucket_policy_decision_for_object(..., None, policy)` (authz.rs:796-806), so request ACL/grant headers are not supplied for policy evaluation. By contrast, `bucket_policy_decision_for_put_object_action` does populate `PolicyRequest` with `.with_canned_acl(...)` and `.with_grant_*` (authz.rs:569-614), showing the intended mechanism exists but is not used for PutObjectAcl. 

Bucket policy evaluation explicitly supports `s3:x-amz-acl` and `s3:x-amz-grant-*` condition keys (bucket_policy.rs:1088-1128) and treats `PutObjectAcl` as an evaluable object action (bucket_policy.rs:593-604). `PolicyRequest` contains fields for canned ACL and grant values (bucket_policy.rs:191-239). However, when those fields are `None`, `StringEquals` conditions do not match (bucket_policy.rs:1133-1157), so deny/allow statements based on `s3:x-amz-acl` or grants are ignored. This confirms the reported bypass: bucket policies that restrict ACL/grant headers on PutObjectAcl are ineffective because the request context is never populated.

